.TH PATCHTEST 1
.SH NAME
patchtest \- regression test for patch(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B patchtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Patchtest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/patch.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(opts,\ patchpath) ,
and checks it against hand-derived expected output for eleven cases,
each built from real files under
.BR /patchtest ,
cleaned up whether or not it fails. Every expected result is derived
directly from the unified diff format's own specification, not copied
from any reference binary \(em see
.IR patch (1)
and
.B patch.b 's
own header comment for why no such binary is available to check
against here.
.PP
Every case uses
.B -d
to chdir to the test directory and bare filenames (no leading
.BR / )
inside the patch text's headers \(em not a style choice:
.IR patch 's
own header parsing strips any leading
.B /
unconditionally, so a header path is never absolute; see
.IR patch (1).
.PP
The two cases most worth calling out specifically, since a single-hunk
test can't reach either:
.TP
.B T3
Exercises
.BR search ()/ searchln ()'s
cross-hunk fuzz carry directly: two hunks in a file that is uniformly
shifted from what the diff assumes, where the second hunk's own
context text appears twice \(em once as a decoy an
.I unadjusted
search would reach first, once at the real, shifted target that only
the fuzz offset carried forward from the first hunk's own match
reaches directly. Confirmed sensitive with a negative control
(temporarily forcing the carried fuzz to 0 made this case edit the
decoy instead \(em see the session notes for the exact derivation).
.TP
.B T10
Exercises
.BR finish ()'s
cleanup-on-failure path: one file's hunk applies cleanly (its
.B .tmp
file gets written) while a second file's hunk fails to match with no
.BR -r ,
so the whole run fails. Checks (via
.BR readdir (2))
that no
.B .tmp
file is left behind afterward, which only happens because
.BR fail ()
now calls
.BR finish (0)
before raising \(em also confirmed sensitive with a negative control
(temporarily reverting that call left a stray
.B .tmp
file, and this check caught it; a plain "is the original file
untouched" check would not have, since
.B blat()
never touches the original either way).
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/patchtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR patch (1)
.SH BUGS
Runs
.I patch
in-process via its
.B run
seam, which takes already-parsed options and a real patch-file path
directly \(em so it does exercise real parsing, searching, and file
I/O, but not
.IR patch 's
own
.BR init ,
its standard-input defaulting, or its window-system
.B Context
handling, none of which
.B run
goes through.
